nodecode-adextract is a rewrite of ad-vertly's ad-vertly/adextract-mcp-server:

  https://github.com/ad-vertly/adextract-mcp-server
  commit 02c08407abe21b4af76b6f066e5edf9490fb231e
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Adextract (LICENSE)

The plugin was a declarative package: a plugin.json with a per-tool cost
table, an mcp.json entry pointing at the hosted MCP server
https://mcp.adextract.co/mcp with a bearer ADEXTRACT_API_KEY, and five skills
(one overview, one per ad library). The server it pointed at advertises
thirteen read-only tools -- four library searches, three advertiser searches,
two advertiser lookups, three single-ad lookups and ad_reader -- and the
plugin held no code at all. This rewrite keeps every one of those tools
reachable, the same endpoint, and the same one-credit-per-call price, and
speaks MCP's streamable HTTP to that endpoint itself through nlk:http (one
initialize, then the tool), so no MCP transport, no Hermes runtime and no
manifest are needed. Because the server serves its own schema at runtime and
the pinned commit carries no copy of it, the tools are grouped by what a
caller wants rather than mirrored one for one: adextract_search,
adextract_advertisers, adextract_advertiser_info, adextract_ad and
adextract_read_creative, each routing on the platform the call names, each
carrying an "extra" member that passes any argument of the library's own tool
through unchanged. The five skills' guidance -- search first, analyze second,
read the top creatives, synthesize format, emotion and funnel role, and the
per-platform gotchas -- is the cell's manual topic, (help :adextract). The
declarative "trust: untrusted" mark is left out: a cell's tools are its own,
and all thirteen were read-only anyway. Nothing of the original is vendored.
